"Passive thermography observes existing temperature patterns. Active thermography applies a controlled thermal stimulus and records the transient response. A subsurface disbond or delamination can alter heat flow and produce a different heating or cooling curve from sound material. Pulsed thermography uses a short heat input; lock-in thermography uses periodic heating and phase/amplitude analysis.",
"Emissivity is a major uncertainty source. A polished metal surface may reflect a nearby hot object and appear warmer than it is. Coatings, viewing angle, focus, camera range, reflected surroundings, wind and thermal boundary conditions affect measurement. A thermal anomaly should be compared with a baseline and, where needed, confirmed by a complementary method."
